abstract: >-
  Modern Software-Defined Vehicles are equipped with a variety of different networks. However, despite the introduction of high-speed alternatives, the CAN bus still prevails in modern architectures for safety-critical components. As part of modern Software-Defined Vehicle development, the verification of those components under targeted bus faults remains an essential step, but often requires expensive testing equipment. This thesis introduces an architecture for a protocol-aware, bus-based CAN fault injector targeting Classical CAN and CAN FD frames, which was implemented and evaluated on the FPGA of an AMD Zynq UltraScale+ MPSoC. Across a set of test vectors, its implementation reliably executed fault injections at data rates up to 1.3 Mbps and supported a high sampling rate, meeting strict timing targets at 240 MHz. Furthermore, it delivered injection capabilities previously limited to proprietary fault injection hardware, allowing testing of the most common bit-level faults, ranging from arbitrary protocol bit overrides and stuff-bit errors up to full CRC or payload corruption. The complete design is open-source and is available on GitHub (https://github.com/Tabzeen/CAN-FD-FI)
